<h1>Attention stats Q&amp;A: why AMX is zero under FPGA attention, and how to read the path-set table</h1>
<p class="cite">Answers to jhan's questions of 2026-10-04 on attn-stats-compare.md (campaign attnstats-20261002, tron main dd0f942c75). Every number below is recomputed from the exit reports in exec/results/attnstats-20261002/rt/ and matches them exactly.</p>

<div class="short">
<p><span class="take">Short version.</span> The AMX kernel scores only whole 64-token pages, so x is 64, not 4 or 16. Under FPGA attention the card scores every key up to the last complete group of four tokens (GOF), and the CPU is left with 1 to 4 keys per step, never a whole page, so the kernel has nothing to take. The 4 and the 16 in your memory are real units, but of other things: 4 tokens is the GOF, the unit the card's K/V copy advances in, and 16 token rows is one AMX tile, four of which make one page.</p>
</div>

<h2>Words used here</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>page</b> = a 64-token block of the KV cache. <b>pending page</b> = the page that the current forward is still writing (it holds the newest 1 to 64 tokens). <b>ready page</b> = a page written before this forward.</li>
<li><b>dense page</b> = a page with all 64 entries live that the query sees through one range and that lies inside the sliding window. Only dense pages take the AMX kernel [h/tron/models/self_attention.hpp:1710-1721 at main].</li>
<li><b>visit</b> = one (token job, KV head, page) scoring step of the software loop. <b>K tokens</b> (keys) = the keys that step scored. The exit report counts both per path: amx, avx, empty.</li>
<li><b>GOF</b> = group of four tokens, the unit in which K/V are copied to the FPGA card [h/tron/gof.hpp:3-5]. <b>DMA</b> = that copy. The card scores keys only up to the end of the unbroken run of complete GOFs (count_complete_prefix_gofs, h/tron/shard.hpp:355-365).</li>
<li><b>AoF</b> = attention on the FPGA (USE_HW_ATTN unset for qwen). <b>software attention</b> = USE_HW_ATTN=0, the CPU scores every key, with the AMX kernel for dense pages and the AVX loop ("dotter") for the rest.</li>
<li><b>decode step s</b> = the s-th forward after the prompt forwards; it scores the query at position p = P + s - 1 (0-based) against positions 0..p, where P is the prompt length. qwen at prompt 1024 has s = 1..255 (255 decode forwards: the first generated token comes out of the last prompt forward).</li>
<li><b>per (user, KV head, layer)</b> = the unit of the closed forms below. The campaign totals are that number times 8 users x 8 KV heads x 36 layers = 2,304 for qwen3-4b.</li>
</ul>

<h2>1. AMX counters under AoF</h2>

<div class="q">Q: at decode, under software attention, AMX functions every x tokens (4 or 16?) until the new tokens fill a page, so AVX should never be zero. Under AoF, AoF functions every 64 tokens and x is less than 64, so both AMX and AVX should have functioned. The report says zero AMX. Please confirm x.</div>

<p><span class="take">x is 64.</span> The kernel's decision unit is the (query, page) pair, and it takes a page only when the whole 64-token page is dense [h/tron/kernels/amx_attn_iface.hpp:19-30; self_attention.hpp:1710-1721]. The K operand of the kernel fills its tiles completely: "64 tokens = 4 tiles of 16 rows" [amx_attn_iface.hpp:27]. A partial page, whatever its fill, goes to the AVX loop.</p>

<p>Where the 4 and the 16 come from:</p>
<ul>
<li>4 tokens = one GOF, the copy unit of the FPGA path. It sets how far the card's coverage reaches at each step, not when the kernel runs.</li>
<li>16 token rows = one AMX tile (16 rows x 64 bytes). The kernel loads four such tiles per page and never runs on fewer than four.</li>
</ul>

<h3>1.1 Software attention on the CPU: what each decode step does</h3>
<p>At step s the query at position p = P + s - 1 scores positions 0..p. The pages below the pending page are full and ready, so each is one AMX visit of 64 keys. The pending page holds (p mod 64) + 1 keys. Two cases:</p>
<ul>
<li>(p mod 64) &lt; 63: the pending page is partial. One AVX visit with (p mod 64) + 1 keys. This is the case in 252 of the 255 steps.</li>
<li>(p mod 64) = 63: the step completes the page inside the pending pass. The page is then dense and the pending pass takes it with the kernel: one AMX visit of 64 keys, zero AVX visits for this step. At P = 1024 this happens at s = 64, 128 and 192 (p = 1087, 1151, 1215).</li>
</ul>
<p>So you are right that AVX is almost never zero under software attention. It is zero only in the three page-completing steps, and the counters show that: the qwen CPU cell has 24 token jobs with path set "amx" only, 8 users x 3 steps.</p>

<h3>1.2 FPGA attention: what each decode step does</h3>
<p>The card does not wait for a page to fill. Its coverage ends at the last complete GOF, so at step s it scores positions 0 .. 4 x floor(p / 4) - 1, and the CPU scores the (p mod 4) + 1 newest keys (1 to 4 keys) with the AVX loop. The DMA of a GOF lands within the step, so in every one of the 255 steps the measured tail was exactly (p mod 4) + 1 keys: no copy lag was seen [sum over p = 1024..1278 of ((p mod 4) + 1) = 636 keys per (user, KV head, layer); measured pending_avx_k_tokens 1,465,344 / 2,304 = 636.0].</p>
<p>The CPU tail is at most 4 keys, so the pending page is never dense on the CPU side, and the kernel takes nothing: ready_amx_visits = 0 and pending_amx_visits = 0 in both arms. The full pages are on the card, which has its own scoring and never calls the kernel.</p>

