<div class="q">Q: how can the FPGA score 100 %?</div>
<p>It does not score exactly 100 %. The table rounds. At prompt 8192 the card scored 4,886,701,056 K tokens (software scale) and the CPU tail 1,465,344, so the card's share is 99.97 %, printed as "100.0 %". At prompt 1024 it is 99.78 %, printed as "99.8 %". The CPU tail is the same 1,465,344 K tokens in both cells, because the tail depends only on p mod 4 and the step count, not on the prompt length. The AVX counter is never zero under AoF: every step has one AVX visit of 1 to 4 keys per (KV head, layer).</p>

<div class="q">Q: what does "2.49 keys" mean? How can it not be a whole number?</div>
<p>It is a mean. Each visit scores a whole number of keys: 1, 2, 3 or 4, following (p mod 4) + 1 as p advances. Over the 255 steps the tail lengths are 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4, ... starting at p = 1024 (1 key), and their sum is 636 keys. 636 / 255 = 2.494 keys per visit. With 256 steps the mean would be exactly 2.5. The report should have said "2.49 keys per visit on average (1 to 4 keys per step)"; the sentence you quoted compressed that.</p>

<div class="q">Q: "The tail never fills a page, so the kernel has nothing to take": does AMX only function on whole pages? Then x = 64.</div>
<p>Yes. Confirmed in the code at main: the predicate requires begin == 0 and end == page_size (64), the whole page visible through one range, and the whole page inside the sliding window [self_attention.hpp:1710-1721]. The kernel comment says the same: "a full 64-token page, all of it visible to the query" [amx_attn_iface.hpp:19-22]. Under AoF the CPU side holds at most 4 keys of the pending page, so the predicate fails at every step.</p>

<div class="q">Q: what is the equation for AMX and AVX scores under software attention after prefill, for decode length L (qwen3-4b)? And under AoF?</div>

<h3>1.3 Equations, per (user, KV head, layer)</h3>
<p>P = prompt length in tokens. S = number of decode forwards = L - 1 for L generated tokens (the first generated token comes out of the last prompt forward). Step s = 1..S scores the query at position p = P + s - 1 against keys 0..p. Floor division is written floor(). The totals of a run are these sums times (users x KV heads x layers) = 8 x 8 x 36 = 2,304 for qwen3-4b.</p>

<p><b>Software attention on the CPU (kernel enabled):</b></p>
<pre>
ready AMX visits      = sum over s = 1..S of floor(p / 64)                  (full pages below the pending page)
pending AMX visits    = number of s in 1..S with (p mod 64) = 63            (steps that complete a page)
AMX K tokens          = 64 x (ready AMX visits + pending AMX visits)
pending AVX visits    = number of s in 1..S with (p mod 64) &lt; 63            (steps with a partial pending page)
AVX K tokens          = sum over those s of ((p mod 64) + 1)
ready AVX visits      = 0                                                   (every ready page is dense for qwen; llama differs, see the report's section 8)
</pre>
<p><b>Software attention with the kill switch (TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1):</b> the same visits with the same key counts, all on the AVX loop. Every former AMX visit becomes one avx_full_page visit of 64 keys. That is the identity the report checks in section 4.</p>

<p><b>Attention on the FPGA (steady state, no copy lag):</b></p>
<pre>
AMX visits            = 0                                                   (the CPU never holds a whole page)
AVX visits            = S                                                   (one pending-pass visit per step)
AVX K tokens          = sum over s = 1..S of ((p mod 4) + 1)                (the tail past the last complete GOF)
card K tokens         = sum over s = 1..S of 4 x floor(p / 4)               (per query; multiply by KV heads for the software scale)
</pre>
<p>These hold when every GOF copy lands within the step, which the data show for both prompt lengths. When a copy lags by whole pages the CPU scores those pages, and if the kernel is enabled the dense ones go to it; the only such case in this campaign is the qwen prompt-8192 prefill (7,077,888 AMX visits, 0.6 % of the prefill K tokens; report section 2.1).</p>

